#pragma once

// Arrangement Builder (Phase 2 Task 5): song structure over generate_candidates.
//
// JUCE-free, pure C++20. Compiled into harmony_forge_inference (it
// orchestrates inference); the core archive itself stays dependency-free.
//
// Model: an Arrangement is an ordered list of Sections laid out sequentially
// in bars. Each section is generated by generate_candidates with the section
// seed (deterministic: same Arrangement + same ArrangeParams -> bit-identical
// Pattern), then shaped by variation/energy, then concatenated in tick space.
//
// Ticks: PPQ is 480 and metre is 4/4, so total_ticks = bars * 4 * 480.
// Bars are explicit (no tempo lives here); the minutes mapping is
// minutes = bars * 4 * 60 / BPM, e.g. 150 bars @ 120 BPM = 5 minutes.
//
// Per-section generation (exact):
// - The section's bars are split into generation chunks of at most 16 bars
//   (the generate_candidates codec cap): 16-bar chunks plus a remainder.
//   Chunk i uses seed mix32(section.seed, i). Chunk starts are multiples of
//   16 bars and therefore 4-bar-phase aligned with the I-V-vi-IV loop, so
//   the section progression below matches the generated harmony exactly.
// - The base generation uses the caller's GenMode (ArrangeParams.base.mode,
//   Strict by default) and the Safe candidate (index 0). The mode is
//   arrangement-wide and lives only in ArrangeParams.base (D-B2: the 5-field
//   arrange SPEC is unchanged, so there is no per-section mode). Energy below
//   still owns the Strict->Creative ORNAMENT blend on top of whatever mode
//   generated the material: e = 0 adds no ORNAMENTS, whatever the mode.
//   That is exactly what the ruling's "e = 0 -> pure Strict" means - it is
//   a statement about the ORNAMENTS, NOT about the generator's mode: a
//   Creative base generates Creative material at e = 0 too (see the in-scale
//   note below). tonic/scale/model_path come from base.
// - Each section restarts the I-V-vi-IV progression loop (section-local bar
//   phase; see arrangement_progression).
//
// Variation (motif carry-over, minimal honest form): the section's bar-0
// melody rhythm (start offsets + durations within the bar) is the template.
// For bar b >= 1, when hash01(section.seed, b) < variation, the bar's melody
// notes are replaced by the template with pitches transposed by
// (root_b - root_0) from the section progression, snapped to (tonic, scale)
// and octave-pulled into the Melody generator register [60, 84] (octave
// moves preserve pitch class, hence scale membership and the motif's scale
// degrees). variation is clamped to 0..1.
//
// Energy mapping (exact, e clamped to 0..1). All three stages are nested in
// e for a fixed seed, so note count and register spread are structurally
// non-decreasing in e (tested for monotonicity):
// - Density: melody/bass notes survive iff they start a bar (beat 0) or
//   hash01(section.seed, bar, start, pitch) < 0.4 + 0.6 * e. Chords always
//   survive (harmonic bed).
// - Register focus: melody notes outside [72-h, 72+h] with h = 6+round(6e)
//   rest (dropped); bass notes outside [42-h, 42+h] with h = 6+round(18e)
//   rest. At e = 1 the windows are [60, 84] / [18, 66], which contain the
//   whole generator ranges, so the stage is a no-op.
// - Strict-vs-Creative blend: e = 0 adds no ornaments. For e > 0 the first
//   floor(e * N) deterministic passing-tone ornaments are added, where N is
//   the number of Creative-rule slots (same-bar note pairs with pitch gap
//   >= 3 whose midpoint lands on a weak 16th-grid tick; semitone approach
//   to the second note, velocity 70, 120 ticks). Slots are computed from the
//   pre-energy melody (identical for all e), respect the Melody register
//   (out-of-register approaches are not slots), and are exempt from
//   thinning. A larger e therefore always keeps a superset of the notes.
//   Note: the ORNAMENTS are weak-beat chromaticism by design (the Creative
//   character), so they take the full in-scale guarantee away from every
//   e > 0. The ORNAMENT blend is independent of base.mode (B2): a Creative
//   base can carry both the generator's own chromatic passing tones and
//   these ornaments.
//
// In-scale guarantee, stated exactly (STRICT vs CREATIVE base):
// - On a STRICT base the material is in scale at e = 0 ONLY: the generator
//   adds no chromaticism of its own, so e = 0 (no ornaments) is in scale,
//   and every e > 0 is not (the ornaments are chromatic by design).
// - On a CREATIVE base the material is OUTSIDE the scale at EVERY e, e = 0
//   included: the generator itself inserts chromatic passing tones
//   (insert_chromatic_passing_tones, src/inference/candidate_modes.cpp)
//   and those are present regardless of the energy.
// The ruling's "e = 0 -> pure Strict" is a statement about the ORNAMENTS
// (e = 0 adds no ornaments), NOT about the generator's mode.
//
// Section transitions get a 1-bar fill treatment: the last bar of every
// non-final section carries 8 eighth-note Drums hits (pitch 38, velocity
// ramp 80 -> 112, 120 ticks each). Drums reuse the existing conventions
// (TrackType::Drums, channel 9 per assign_default_channels, unpitched map
// excluded from pitched analysis); no new semantics are introduced.
//
// Key continuity: a single tonic/scale from ArrangeParams.base spans the
// whole arrangement (multi-key sections are out of scope and unexpressible:
// Section carries no key). Concatenation preserves the per-section
// resolve_harmonic_conflicts guarantees; hanging notes are impossible by
// construction (per-section patterns are shifted, never stretched, and each
// already satisfies start + duration <= section end), debug-asserted.
//
// Empty arrangement: returns a Pattern (480 PPQ, 4/4) with three empty
// typed tracks and an empty progression. Section bars <= 0 are clamped to
// 1 (total_bars uses the same clamp, so layout math always agrees).
//
// Never throws; pure data orchestration (no audio-thread contact).

#include <optional>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>
#include <vector>

#include "candidates.h"
#include "core/model/note.h"

namespace hf::arr {

enum class SectionType {
  Intro,
  Verse,
  PreChorus,
  Chorus,
  Bridge,
  Breakdown,
  Buildup,
  Drop,
  Outro
};

/// Display name ("Intro", "Verse", ..., never null).
const char* section_type_name(SectionType t);

/// Case-insensitive parse of the 9 display names; nullopt otherwise.
std::optional<SectionType> section_type_from_name(std::string_view name);

struct Section {
  SectionType type = SectionType::Verse;
  int bars = 4;
  float energy = 0.5f;     ///< 0..1 (clamped)
  float variation = 0.0f;  ///< 0..1 (clamped)
  unsigned seed = 0;
};

struct Arrangement {
  std::vector<Section> sections;

  /// Sum of per-section bars with the <= 0 -> 1 clamp applied.
  int total_bars() const;
};

struct ArrangeParams {
  // base.mode is the arrangement-wide generation mode (D-B2). Strict is the
  // default, so an untouched ArrangeParams reproduces the pre-B2 bytes;
  // Creative/Experimental come straight from the caller (nothing overrides
  // them). Energy (per section) keeps blending the Creative ORNAMENTS on top,
  // independent of this mode; on a Creative base the material is outside the
  // scale at every e (the generator's own chromatic passing tones), whatever
  // the energy.
  hf::infer::GenParams base;
};

/// Build the full arrangement Pattern (tracks Melody, Bass, Chords plus a
/// Drums fill track when there is at least one section transition).
core::Pattern build_arrangement(const Arrangement& arr, const ArrangeParams& params);

/// Bar-indexed chord progression behind build_arrangement (one chord per
/// bar, sections concatenated, each restarting the I-V-vi-IV loop with the
/// fallback-to-major rule of generation). Length == total_bars().
core::ChordProgression arrangement_progression(const Arrangement& arr,
                                               const ArrangeParams& params);

/// Minimal spec format: "Type:bars:energy:variation:seed,...", e.g.
/// "Intro:4:0.3:0.2:7,Verse:8:0.5:0.3:7,Chorus:8:0.9:0.4:8".
/// Strict: every entry needs all 5 fields; bars must be 1..512; energy and
/// variation must be finite numbers (clamped to 0..1); at most 64 sections.
/// Anything else (including the empty string) -> nullopt. Never throws.
std::optional<Arrangement> parse_arrange_spec(std::string_view spec);

}  // namespace hf::arr
